I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish a reply whose ```json fence contains an object amid extra prose from a reply that is only the later top-level object. The documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” Reply 1 is the string "```json\nprefix {\"a\":1} suffix\n```\n{\"a\":2}". Reply 2 is the string "{\"a\":2}". For Reply 1, the rule requires the check to return {"a":1}, because a code fence holds the object {"a":1} and that fenced object is the last one inside a fence. For Reply 2, the rule requires the check to return {"a":2}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {"a":2}. The check as written will return {"a":2} for both: its fenced pool tries to parse the entire fence body "prefix {\"a\":1} suffix" as one JSON object, fails, and then the top-level pool returns the later {"a":2}. What would make me wrong is if the check actually returns {"a":1} for Reply 1, or if the rule is read to require the fence body itself to be exactly one JSON object rather than merely to contain one, in which case Reply 1 would not have a fenced object and the pair would not be a discrepancy.